      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor of two numbers, which is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference.\n\nWe have two numbers: 1407 and 903. \n\nThe Euclidean algorithm goes as follows:\n\n1. Divide the larger number by the smaller number and denote the remainder as r. In our case, we divide 1407 by 903:\n\n   1407 = 903 × 1 + r\n\n   Given that the remainder r is greater than zero (in this case, r = 504), we continue with the algorithm.\n\n   903 = 504 × 1 + 399\n\n2. Now we take the smaller number (which is now 504) and divide it by the remainder (399) from the previous step:\n\n   504 = 399 × 1 + 105\n\n3. Continue the process by taking the remainder obtained from the above division (105) and dividing it by the current smaller number.\n\n   399 = 105 × 3 + 94\n\n4. When the remainder is less than or equal to the divisor (in our case 105), we stop. In our example, the remainder is not less than 105, so we repeat step 3 until we get a remainder that is less than or equal to the divisor.\n\n5. Repeat steps 2 and 3 if the remainder is still greater than the divisor. Now the remainder is either the GCD of the original two numbers or a smaller multiple of the original divisor. Once the remainder is less than or equal to the divisor, stop the process.\n\nNow, we can write the GCD as the largest number between the numbers that produced the remainder. In our example, this is 105.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903 is $\\boxed{105}$.",
